Downloads will keep running — they're independent. Let me check your current lineup and then research what's new, since my training cutoff predates today by about six months:

Two things to flag before the research: your `ollama list` now shows **only qwen3.5:4b and 9b** — the `35b` and all three `qwen3.5-srini:*` persona tags are gone, re-created 10 minutes ago with new IDs. I'll come back to that.

For the model question, let me check the live Ollama library rather than trusting listicles:

Now the decisive data — exact tags and sizes for the candidates that could fit 8 GB:

Here's what's actually new and fits your card, with real sizes from the live Ollama library (not listicles).

## Your constraint

**6.6 GB usable** — 8188 MiB nameplate minus the ~1.4 GB the Windows compositor holds. Weights plus KV cache must fit under that.

## The honest headline: your 9B is still the right daily driver

Qwen3.5-9B at Q4_K_M (6.6 GB, 256K context, BFCL-V4 66.1) is still what the 8 GB tier roundups pick as of October–November 2026. Nothing released since beats it *at this size*. So this isn't a "you're behind" situation.

## What's genuinely worth adding

| model | size | context | why it's interesting |
|---|---|---|---|
| **`granite4.2:8b`** | **5.3 GB** | 128K | IBM, Apache 2.0, built specifically for **tool use, RAG and structured JSON output**. 1.3 GB lighter than your 9B, so more room for KV cache — which matters for zimrag's multi-source prompts |
| **`gemma4:e2b-it-qat`** | **4.3 GB** | 128K | Google, **vision + audio input** (you have no multimodal model today). QAT weights, so better quality per byte than plain Q4 |
| **`granite4.2:3b`** | **2.2 GB** | 128K | Fast triage/classification — the job I suggested for your 4B, done cheaper |

## What looks tempting but doesn't fit

- **`nemotron-3.5-bolt:e4b`** — NVIDIA's compact coding/tool model, but the Q4_K_M tag is **7.9 GB** and even the nvfp4 is 7.2 GB. Over your 6.6 GB ceiling, and nvfp4 wants Blackwell anyway — your 4060 Ti is Ada.
- **`gemma4:e4b-it-q4_K_M`** — 6.6 GB, exactly your whole budget with nothing left for cache. The `e4b-it-qat` tag at 6.1 GB is the only version worth trying, and it's still tight.
- **`qwen3.8`** — the real successor to your line, but library-only at **27b / 18 GB**. There are community 9B distills (`kiwi_kiwi/qwen3.8-abliterated-9b`, `eriktonon/qwen3.8-uncensored:9b`), but they're unofficial *abliterated* builds with safety training stripped and no provenance — which cuts against your own stance that pins and provenance are security boundaries. I'd skip them.

## Replacing the 35B wildcard

`nemotron-3.5-lightning:30b` is the closest fit: a 30B MoE with only **3B active parameters**, explicitly built for "always-on agents" with tools and thinking. With 64 GB RAM, CPU offload stays usable precisely because so little is active per token — the same trick that made your 35B tolerable.

Also just landed (10 hours ago): `inkling-2-mini` from Thinking Machines — 26B total, 5.4B active, looped layers. Too new to have any track record.

## Two things you should know

**Your model store lost tags.** `ollama list` shows only `qwen3.5:4b` and `9b`, both re-created 10 minutes ago with new IDs. **`qwen3.5:35b` and all three `qwen3.5-srini:*` persona tags are gone.** Rebuilding the persona tags is one command — `python profile\build_persona_models.py --model 4b --model 9b` — but it'll fail on the 35b target until you re-pull that base, so pass `--model` explicitly or re-pull `qwen3.5:35b` first.

**Pulling now competes with the ZIM downloads** for bandwidth. `granite4.2:3b` is only 2.2 GB if you want a quick compatibility check that Ollama 0.40.17 handles the Granite 4.2 architecture; the rest I'd queue until Gutenberg finishes.

## What I'd actually do

You have the rig to settle this empirically instead of trusting my table. `grade_student.py` plus `dataset/code_eval.jsonl` already gives you a 60-question benchmark, and zimrag gives you grounded-answer tests. Say the word and I'll pull `granite4.2:8b` and `gemma4:e2b-it-qat`, then score them head-to-head against `qwen3.5:9b` on both — your own prompts, your own hardware, with tok/s measured. That's a far better basis than anyone's leaderboard.

