I will use the `mcp__zimrag__zim_search` tool to find the documentation for the `git reset --hard` command.



The `git reset --hard` command is the most powerful reset, as it writes to the current branch name, resets the index to match the current commit, and **clobbers whatever changes are in the working tree**. This means any uncommitted changes in the working directory are permanently lost.

The command is described as: "It first writes a new hash ID into the current branch name, which if we didn't say which one to use, is the old hash ID and leaves the branch name unchanged. Then it uses whatever commit the branch name now identifies to re-set the index. Whatever we had staged is gone. Last, it writes to the work-tree . 1" [1].

The main risk is that it destroys any uncommitted work in the working directory.

